<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Idrisi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–c. 1165</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as al-Idrisi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad al-Idrisi was an Arab geographer and cartographer, born probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, who worked at the court of the Norman king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. In 1154 he completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-rogeriana">Tabula Rogeriana</a>, or Book of Roger, a description of the known world with a world map divided into climatic zones. It was one of the most detailed geographical works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Idrisi" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mario Sánchez Bueno; derivative work by Ecemaml · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_de_Al-Idrisi_bajo_el_baluarte_de_los_Mallorquines,_Ceuta_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Idrisi came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, which claimed descent from Muhammad through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>. He is believed to have been born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1100, at that time held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, where his great-grandfather had been forced to settle after Hammudid Málaga fell to the Zirids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. He spent much of his youth travelling through North Africa and Al-Andalus and acquired detailed information on both regions. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> when he was barely sixteen and studied at the university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. His travels also took him to Portugal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, the French Atlantic coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and Jórvík, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Because of conflict and instability in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, al-Idrisi joined contemporaries such as Abu al-Salt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> had overthrown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> who had been loyal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. He drew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-rogeriana">Tabula Rogeriana</a> in 1154 for King Roger II after a stay of eighteen years at the court, during which he worked on the commentaries and illustrations of the map. Its legends are in Arabic. It shows the Eurasian continent in its entirety but only the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and it lacks detail for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. For Roger the map was also inscribed on a massive disc of solid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> two metres in diameter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Al-Idrisi combined knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the Far East gathered by Islamic merchants and explorers, recorded on Islamic maps, with the information brought by Norman voyagers. The map illustrates his Kitab nuzhat al-mushtaq, which may be translated A Diversion for the Man Longing to Travel to Far-Off Places. It survives in nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, seven of them with maps. In its introduction he names Ptolemy and an astronomer, probably Ishaq ibn al-Hasan al-Zayyat, as sources for geographical coordinates and says he cross-checked oral reports from different informants. It records Chinese trade, mentioning the glassware of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>. He also compiled a medical dictionary listing drugs and plants with their names in as many as twelve languages.</p><p>His work influenced Islamic scholars including Ibn Sa'id al-Maghribi, Hafiz-i Abru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, but it was unknown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> and had little effect on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> cartography. An abridged Arabic text was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1592, one of the first Arabic books ever printed. An abridged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1619, and a complete French translation by Pierre Amédée Jaubert in the middle of the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad al-Idrisi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Arab geographer <b>al-Idrisi</b> completed his great world geography, the Book of Roger, for Roger II in 1154, and translations of Greek and Arabic works into Latin were made at the court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The geographer <b>al-Idrisi</b>, writing in the 1150s, mentions Mombasa as a small town on the coast of the Zanj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Reval, the medieval name of Tallinn, grew on a site long used by the Estonians, who had a fort on the limestone hill of Toompea and a harbour that the Arab geographer <b>al-Idrisi</b> probably refers to in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Song merchant shipping carried iron, swords, silk, velvet, porcelain and textiles across the Indian Ocean; the Moroccan geographer <b>al-Idrisi</b> wrote in 1154 of the prowess of Chinese merchant ships there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1926 Hasan <b>al-Idrisi</b> accepted a Saudi protectorate; Ibn Saud annexed the territory in 1930, and after a failed revolt and the Saudi-Yemeni war of 1934 it was absorbed into Saudi Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>A harbour and stronghold at Lindanise served Baltic shipping, and the Arab geographer <b>al-Idrisi's</b> 1154 map may record it as a town called Qlwry.</blockquote><a href="/state/ravala">Rävala</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in Sicily, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.89,-5.3&amp;year=1142" aria-label="Ceuta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad al-Idrisi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4633 4489h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4633 4489h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.89,-5.3&amp;year=1142">What was here in 1142? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
